  "team-effectiveness": {
    "lessons": [
      {
        "id": "team-effectiveness-1",
        "module": 0,
        "title": "Welcome to the course",
        "type": "video",
        "url": "assets/materials/team-effectiveness/welcome.mp4",
        "fileName": "welcome.mp4",
        "body":
            "A short welcome to Team Effectiveness: how the course works and how to earn your certificate.",
        "minutes": 1,
      },
      {
        "id": "team-effectiveness-2",
        "module": 0,
        "title":
            "Watch: How to turn a group of strangers into a team (Amy Edmondson, TED)",
        "type": "video",
        "url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3boKz0Exros",
        "fileName": "",
        "body":
            "Watch this talk and note one idea you can use in your work. It supports the module \"Understanding Team Dynamics\".",
        "minutes": 15,
      },
      {
        "id": "team-effectiveness-3",
        "module": 0,
        "title": "Reading: Understanding Team Dynamics",
        "type": "reading",
        "url": "",
        "fileName": "",
        "body":
            "Team dynamics are the unwritten patterns of how people work together. Teams move through forming, storming, norming and performing, and leaders can speed this up with clear goals and norms.\n\nKey ideas\n\u2022 Every team develops norms; make the important ones explicit.\n\u2022 Conflict in the storming stage is normal and can be productive.\n\u2022 Roles and goals should be clear to every member.\n\nTry this\nAgree three team norms (for example how you run meetings and share updates) and write them down.",
        "minutes": 10,
      },
      {
        "id": "team-effectiveness-4",
        "module": 0,
        "title": "Course slides",
        "type": "slides",
        "url": "assets/materials/team-effectiveness/slides.pptx",
        "fileName": "slides.pptx",
        "body":
            "Slide deck covering every module. Open it full screen or download it for later.",
        "minutes": 15,
      },
      {
        "id": "team-effectiveness-5",
        "module": 1,
        "title":
            "Watch: Building a psychologically safe workplace (Amy Edmondson, TEDxHGSE)",
        "type": "video",
        "url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LhoLuui9gX8",
        "fileName": "",
        "body":
            "Watch this talk and note one idea you can use in your work. It supports the module \"Building Trust\".",
        "minutes": 15,
      },
      {
        "id": "team-effectiveness-6",
        "module": 1,
        "title": "Reading: Building Trust",
        "type": "reading",
        "url": "",
        "fileName": "",
        "body":
            "Psychological safety is the shared belief that it is safe to take interpersonal risks: to ask questions, admit mistakes and offer ideas. It was the strongest predictor of team effectiveness in Google's research.\n\nKey ideas\n\u2022 Frame work as learning problems, not just execution.\n\u2022 Acknowledge your own fallibility as a leader.\n\u2022 Respond to bad news with appreciation, not blame.\n\nTry this\nAt your next meeting, ask \"What might we be missing?\" and thank the first person who answers.",
        "minutes": 10,
      },
      {
        "id": "team-effectiveness-7",
        "module": 1,
        "title": "Learner workbook",
        "type": "pdf",
        "url": "assets/materials/team-effectiveness/workbook.pdf",
        "fileName": "workbook.pdf",
        "body":
            "Summaries, key ideas, practice activities and space for your notes for each module.",
        "minutes": 20,
      },
      {
        "id": "team-effectiveness-8",
        "module": 2,
        "title": "Watch: Build a tower, build a team (Tom Wujec, TED)",
        "type": "video",
        "url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=H0_yKBitO8M",
        "fileName": "",
        "body":
            "Watch this talk and note one idea you can use in your work. It supports the module \"Collaboration and Cooperation\".",
        "minutes": 15,
      },
      {
        "id": "team-effectiveness-9",
        "module": 2,
        "title": "Reading: Collaboration and Cooperation",
        "type": "reading",
        "url": "",
        "fileName": "",
        "body":
            "Collaboration works when people share information early, test ideas quickly and build on each other's strengths rather than competing.\n\nKey ideas\n\u2022 Prototype and test early instead of planning in isolation.\n\u2022 Make work visible so others can help.\n\u2022 Combine different skills: diversity improves problem solving.\n\nTry this\nShare a work-in-progress draft with two colleagues before it is finished and ask for one improvement each.",
        "minutes": 10,
      },
      {
        "id": "team-effectiveness-10",
        "module": 2,
        "title": "Reflection worksheet",
        "type": "document",
        "url": "assets/materials/team-effectiveness/worksheet.docx",
        "fileName": "worksheet.docx",
        "body":
            "Download the Word worksheet, answer the reflection questions and keep it for your records.",
        "minutes": 15,
      },
      {
        "id": "team-effectiveness-11",
        "module": 3,
        "title": "Watch: The walk from \"no\" to \"yes\" (William Ury, TED)",
        "type": "video",
        "url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Hc6yi_FtoNo",
        "fileName": "",
        "body":
            "Watch this talk and note one idea you can use in your work. It supports the module \"Managing Team Conflict\".",
        "minutes": 15,
      },
      {
        "id": "team-effectiveness-12",
        "module": 3,
        "title": "Reading: Managing Team Conflict",
        "type": "reading",
        "url": "",
        "fileName": "",
        "body":
            "Task conflict about ideas can improve decisions; relationship conflict about people damages teams. Address tension early and keep the focus on shared goals.\n\nKey ideas\n\u2022 Distinguish task conflict from relationship conflict.\n\u2022 Use ground rules: one speaker at a time, critique ideas not people.\n\u2022 Return to the shared goal when discussions stall.\n\nTry this\nMap a current disagreement: what each side wants, why, and what goal you share.",
        "minutes": 10,
      },
      {
        "id": "team-effectiveness-13",
        "module": 3,
        "title":
            "Further reading: High-performing teams need psychological safety (Harvard Business Review)",
        "type": "link",
        "url":
            "https://hbr.org/2017/08/high-performing-teams-need-psychological-safety-heres-how-to-create-it",
        "fileName": "",
        "body":
            "Read this article to go deeper into the ideas from this module.",
        "minutes": 10,
      },
      {
        "id": "team-effectiveness-14",
        "module": 4,
        "title":
            "Watch: Forget the pecking order at work (Margaret Heffernan, TED)",
        "type": "video",
        "url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Vyn_xLrtZaY",
        "fileName": "",
        "body":
            "Watch this talk and note one idea you can use in your work. It supports the module \"High-Performance Teams\".",
        "minutes": 15,
      },
      {
        "id": "team-effectiveness-15",
        "module": 4,
        "title": "Reading: High-Performance Teams",
        "type": "reading",
        "url": "",
        "fileName": "",
        "body":
            "High-performing teams combine psychological safety, dependability, clear structure, meaningful work and a sense of impact. They review and improve how they work, not just what they deliver.\n\nKey ideas\n\u2022 Dependability: members deliver quality work on time.\n\u2022 Structure and clarity: goals, roles and plans are clear.\n\u2022 Meaning and impact: people see why their work matters.\n\nTry this\nScore your team from 1 to 5 on the five dynamics and choose one to improve this month.",
        "minutes": 10,
      },
      {
        "id": "team-effectiveness-16",
        "module": 4,
        "title": "Action plan",
        "type": "pdf",
        "url": "assets/materials/team-effectiveness/action-plan.pdf",
        "fileName": "action-plan.pdf",
        "body":
            "Turn what you learned into specific actions with dates. Print it or fill it in on screen.",
        "minutes": 10,
      },
    ],
    "quiz": [
      {
        "type": "single",
        "prompt":
            "What was the strongest predictor of team effectiveness in Google's Project Aristotle?",
        "options": [
          "Psychological safety",
          "Team size",
          "Office location",
          "Seniority of members",
        ],
        "answers": ["Psychological safety"],
        "explanation": "Psychological safety mattered most.",
      },
      {
        "type": "single",
        "prompt": "Which stage comes after forming in Tuckman's model?",
